Step-by-step explanation:

1. We first sort the fields by threat level (highest to lowest). Their threat levels are:
 • Field_2: 0.09
 • Field_3: 0.06
 • Field_4: 0.06
 • Field_1: 0.03

2. For the most threatened field (Field_2), the full protection requires 4 drones. There are currently no drones already protecting Field_2.

3. We then sort all the idle drones by their distance to Field_2. Approximating Field_2’s center as ((6+22)/2, (30+45)/2) = (14, 37.5) we get:
 • Drone_1 (8,36) ~6.2 units away
 • Drone_8 (6,31) ~10.3 units away
 • Drone_5 (28,30) ~15.9 units away
 • Drone_4 (31,48) ~20.0 units away
 • Drone_6 (41,24) ~30.2 units away
 • Drone_3 (16,7) ~30.6 units away
 • Drone_7 (50,13) ~43.6 units away
 • Drone_2 (48,4) ~47.7 units away

 Thus, we take the closest four drones to Field_2 – Drone_1, Drone_8, Drone_5, and Drone_4 – and assign them to "protecting Field_2".

4. The remaining drones are Drone_2, Drone_3, Drone_6, and Drone_7. Next, we distribute these among the other fields. Following the order of threat levels after Field_2:
 • Field_3 (threat 0.06) needs 2 drones. We check distances from Field_3’s center (calculated as ((35+43)/2, (32+48)/2) = (39,40)). The two drones closest among the remaining are Drone_6 and Drone_7, so we assign them to "protecting Field_3".
 • Field_4 (also at threat 0.06) needs 3 drones for full protection but only 2 remain. We assign the remaining drones, Drone_2 and Drone_3, to "protecting Field_4".
 • Field_1, which is the least threatened, does not receive any drones.

5. All drones are now assigned into exactly one of the groups.

The final assignments are listed above.
